  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Wednesday/...\nFor the weekend, the active weather pattern will persist at least\nthrough Saturday, as lingering low pressure in the northern Gulf\nwill keep onshore flow, mainly cloudy skies and rain showers across\nmuch of the Panhandle. As this low weakens, another low passing \nwell to the south will allow a high pressure ridge to build over \nthe Gulf into SEAK. This transition is expected to bring a brief \nlull in rain showers later Sunday.\n\nThe break will be short-lived as another low and front quickly\nswing through the Gulf into SEAK on Monday, likely reaching\nYakutat by the afternoon and then swinging through the panhandle\nMonday night into early Tuesday. With the quick progression of\nthis system, overall rain amounts will be limited, although a \nbrief period of moderate to heavy rain expected with the frontal \npassage. Another large, gale force to potential storm force, low \nwill lift northeast into the Gulf of Alaska on Monday night into \nTuesday, sending a another front and deep moisture feed toward \nSEAK. While models agree on the overall general pattern, \ndifferences in timing and strength of this low and attendant front\nstill persist. Despite model differences, the low is expected to \nstall over northern Gulf and weaken by midweek. However, the \nprolonged onshore flow and a good potential for moderate to heavy \nrain persists behind the initial front through midweek. \n\nWinds will begin to pick up on Monday night as the front\napproaches, with breezy to windy southerlies ahead and with the \nfrontal passage Tuesday, with gales possible for Clarence and\nnorthern Lynn. \n\nCooler daytime temps will prevail with the persistent onshore \nflow, mainly cloudy skies and rain, with highs generally in the\nmid to upper 40s. Nighttime lows in the upper 30s to low 40s.",
